WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.850
Our office cupboard holds registers

2
00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.710
from nineteen ninety-eight.

3
00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:08.130
The old computer still holds every

4
00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.310
pupil's photo since twenty ten.

5
00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.350
Nobody decided to keep them, and

6
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.210
nobody decided to delete them.

7
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.590
Today: how long to keep school

8
00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:21.350
records, how to archive them, and

9
00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.150
how to delete them on purpose.

10
00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:26.170
Every kind of record needs

11
00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:27.990
a keep-until rule.

12
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.740
Some rules come from the law or the

13
00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:32.980
ministry, like how long to keep exam

14
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:33.280
results.

15
00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:35.890
The rest you decide by the purpose:

16
00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:37.890
why do we still need this?

17
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.320
A pupil's final results may be

18
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:42.620
needed for many years.

19
00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:45.650
Daily registers, perhaps a few.

20
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.350
Photos for a sports-day display,

21
00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:50.330
until the display comes down.

22
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:53.740
Write each rule in a table: the record,

23
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.120
how long, why, and who deletes it.

24
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.360
Records you keep must survive.

25
00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.870
Use the three-two-one rule: three

26
00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:07.010
copies, on two kinds of storage, and

27
00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.470
one away from the school.

28
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.380
A fire or a stolen laptop

29
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.240
shouldn't take the only copy.

30
00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:15.170
And test it.

31
00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.320
Once a term, restore one file

32
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.060
from the backup, and open it.

33
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.260
A backup you've never restored

34
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.360
is a hope, not a backup.

35
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.180
For records you keep for years,

36
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.460
choose open formats: CSV for

37
00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.480
tables, PDF for documents.

38
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.420
They'll still open in twenty years.

39
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.660
Compress them, and a whole school's term

40
00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:41.040
of attendance fits in a few kilobytes.

41
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:42.720
Quick check!

42
00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.030
Which of these backups follows

43
00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.990
the three-two-one rule?

44
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:50.180
Three copies, on the computer

45
00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.380
and on a USB drive, with one

46
00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:54.540
kept away from the school.

47
00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:57.250
If the office floods, the copy

48
00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.610
across town survives.

49
00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:01.480
When the keep-until date

50
00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.420
comes, delete on purpose.

51
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.620
Empty the bin, wipe old phones

52
00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.360
and computers before giving them

53
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.500
away, and shred paper.

54
00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:12.590
Then write in the log what

55
00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:14.090
was deleted, and when.

56
00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.030
A query can find what's due.

57
00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.430
Select the records whose year, plus the

58
00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.090
years to keep them, is before this year.

59
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.360
That's your deletion list for the term.

60
00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:28.910
In the app, compare a school's

61
00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.170
term of attendance as CSV, JSON

62
00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.470
and columns, squeezed or not.

63
00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.550
Which would you archive, and which would

64
00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:38.950
you send over a slow connection?

65
00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:41.090
Your turn.

66
00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.990
We've copied the marks to a USB drive

67
00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.370
every week for a year, but never opened

68
00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:47.590
one.

69
00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:49.630
What do we really have?

70
00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:52.670
A hope, until you restore

71
00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:53.810
a file and open it.

72
00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:56.310
Remember: give every record

73
00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:57.970
a keep-until rule.

74
00:02:58.590 --> 00:02:59.490
Back up with

75
00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.050
three-two-one, in open formats.

76
00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:05.630
And delete on purpose, on

77
00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.350
time, and write it down.

78
00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.060
Try it in class tomorrow:

79
00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:12.120
play “Keep or delete?”.

80
00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.830
Give groups cards of school

81
00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.810
records: registers, test papers,

82
00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.950
sports-day photos, report cards.

83
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.860
For each, they decide how

84
00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:25.940
long it's needed and why.

85
00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:28.310
Then compare with your

86
00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:29.830
school's real rules.
